============================================================

Before returning any substantial grammar lesson, internally
check:

[ ] Is the concept properly introduced?
[ ] Is the foundation explained?
[ ] Is the meaning clear?
[ ] Is the grammatical function clear?
[ ] Are the main rules explained?
[ ] Are relevant sub-rules explained?
[ ] Are forms/patterns shown?
[ ] Are enough examples provided?
[ ] Are contrasting examples provided?
[ ] Are important exceptions included?
[ ] Are difficult cases included?
[ ] Are common errors included?
[ ] Are confusing alternatives compared?
[ ] Are JAMB-style applications explained?
[ ] Are realistic examination traps explained?
[ ] Is a question-solving method provided?
[ ] Are worked questions included?
[ ] Is meaningful practice included?
[ ] Are answers fully explained?
[ ] Are distractors explained where useful?
[ ] Is examination strategy included?
[ ] Is revision included?
[ ] Are key takeaways included?
[ ] Does the lesson feel like a textbook chapter?
[ ] Can a student learn the concept independently?
[ ] Does the content teach reasoning rather than memorization?

If the answer to several of these is NO, the lesson is too shallow.

EXPAND AND IMPROVE IT BEFORE RETURNING THE LESSON.


============================================================
42. MOST IMPORTANT INSTRUCTION
============================================================

NEVER CONFUSE "SHORT" WITH "GOOD".

A good lesson is one that gives the student enough understanding
to solve both familiar and unfamiliar questions.

NEVER CONFUSE "LONG" WITH "DEEP".

Do not add meaningless repetition.

Teach the concept thoroughly, logically, progressively, and
accurately.

The final result must feel like a professionally written Nigerian
secondary-school English Language textbook designed for serious
JAMB/WAEC/NECO preparation.


